# Cron jobs.
#
# THIS IS A TEMPLATE. Do not edit the installed crontab on the server, and do
# not edit this file on the server either: deploy/deploy.sh renders it
# (swapping __CRON_HEADER_FILE__ for the path of a file holding the
# X-Cron-Secret header, and __HEARTBEAT__ for an optional outside ping) and
# pipes it through `crontab -` on every deploy, so any hand edit is silently
# overwritten on the next release. Change it here, in the repo, and deploy.
#
# The header comes from that file (`curl -H @file`), never from the command
# line: a command line is readable by every process on the server through ps,
# for as long as the curl runs.
#
# Cron is served on 127.0.0.1:8081 only (docker/caddy/Caddyfile); the public
# site answers /cron with 404.
#
# Every /cron endpoint is idempotent and is rejected with 403 unless the
# X-Cron-Secret header matches CRON_SECRET, so a missed run is harmless and an
# overlapping run does no damage.
#
# curl -sf: quiet, and a non-2xx response is a non-zero exit. Output is
# discarded, so every job must record its own outcome somewhere a person looks
# (a log table, the inbox) — a job that fails here otherwise goes nowhere.
#
# Every line carries the reason for its interval. Add jobs below this one.

MAILTO=""
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# The cron path and the database, end to end, then the queue (docs/queue.md):
# finished jobs pruned, and a 503 if a due job has waited too long because the
# worker stopped. Every five minutes because it is what pings the heartbeat:
# the outside service alerts when the pings stop, so a stopped worker alerts too.
*/5 * * * * curl -sf -H @__CRON_HEADER_FILE__ http://127.0.0.1:8081/cron/health >/dev/null 2>&1 && curl -sf -H @__CRON_HEADER_FILE__ http://127.0.0.1:8081/cron/queue >/dev/null 2>&1__HEARTBEAT__

# Form submissions past the form_retention_days setting are deleted: they
# are personal data (docs/forms.md).
45 2 * * * curl -sf -H @__CRON_HEADER_FILE__ http://127.0.0.1:8081/cron/forms >/dev/null 2>&1

# The nightly database dump (tools/backup-prod-db.sh keeps the newest 14). Its
# output is kept, because "did last night's backup work" has to be answerable.
15 3 * * * cd /opt/waffle && doppler run -- ./tools/backup-prod-db.sh >>/opt/waffle/.deploy/backup.log 2>&1
